  "worked_solution": "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3\\sqrt{t}-1)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{3/2}-t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =2(1)^{3/2}-1+C \\\\ 1 & =1+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{3/2}-t.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{3/2}-t & =0 \\\\ t(2\\sqrt{t}-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we discard the solution $t=0.$ Therefore, the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{s}.$",
